<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 61 0 R
/T (When a process moves between CPUs, some of the cache state it previously used may no longer be local to its new CPU. Cache-coherence traffic and memory access latency can affect performance. The experiment does not directly measure cache misses. Where permissions and hardware support permit, perf stat can collect cpu-migrations and cache-related counters as additional evidence.)
/E (When a process moves between CPUs, some of the cache state it previously used may no longer be local to its new CPU. Cache-coherence traffic and memory access latency can affect performance. The experiment does not directly measure cache misses. Where permissions and hardware support permit, perf stat can collect cpu-migrations and cache-related counters as additional evidence.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [6]
>>
endobj
91 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 61 0 R
/T (Modern multi-core processors provide several logical CPUs, but performance depends on both where worker processes execute and how tasks are assigned. Linux scheduling normally balances runnable tasks while attempting to preserve useful processor/cache locality. Explicit CPU affinity restricts the CPUs on which a process may run. This can reduce migration and preserve cache locality, but overly restrictive pinning can prevent the scheduler from using otherwise idle CPUs.)
/E (Modern multi-core processors provide several logical CPUs, but performance depends on both where worker processes execute and how tasks are assigned. Linux scheduling normally balances runnable tasks while attempting to preserve useful processor/cache locality. Explicit CPU affinity restricts the CPUs on which a process may run. This can reduce migration and preserve cache locality, but overly restrictive pinning can prevent the scheduler from using otherwise idle CPUs.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [7]
>>
endobj
92 0 obj
<<
/Type /StructElem
/S /H3
/P 61 0 R
/T (Task distribution creates a second trade-off. Static block distribution has low coordination overhead but may leave some workers with disproportionately expensive tasks. Round-robin distribution spreads task positions across workers. Dynamic distribution assigns small chunks from a shared queue, which can improve balance but adds queue coordination overhead. The experiment investigates the combined effect of placement and distribution on a repeatable CPU-bound workload.)
/E (Task distribution creates a second trade-off. Static block distribution has low coordination overhead but may leave some workers with disproportionately expensive tasks. Round-robin distribution spreads task positions across workers. Dynamic distribution assigns small chunks from a shared queue, which can improve balance but adds queue coordination overhead. The experiment investigates the combined effect of placement and distribution on a repeatable CPU-bound workload.)
/Pg 11 0 R
/K [8]
>>
endobj
