1036 0 obj
<<
/Title (A controlled CPU-bound workload is executed by multiple Python worker processes. Three affinity modes are compared: none \(the Linux scheduler chooses placement\), spread \(each worker is restricted to one available logical CPU\), and single \(all workers are restricted to one available logical CPU as a parallelism-reduction control\). Three distribution strategies are compared: block, round-robin \(rr\), and dynamic shared-queue distribution. The program records elapsed time, throughput, worker-time imbalance, worker CPU time, and per-worker context-switch counters exposed by /proc/self/status. Optional Linux tools provide independent process, scheduler, and migration observations.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 829 0 R
/Prev 964 0 R
/Next 965 0 R
>>
endobj
1037 0 obj
<<
/Title (Modern multi-core processors provide several logical CPUs, but performance depends on both where worker processes execute and how tasks are assigned. Linux scheduling normally balances runnable tasks while attempting to preserve useful processor/cache locality. Explicit CPU affinity restricts the CPUs on which a process may run. This can reduce migration and preserve cache locality, but overly restrictive pinning can prevent the scheduler from using otherwise idle CPUs.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 966 0 R
/Next 1038 0 R
>>
endobj
1038 0 obj
<<
/Title (Task distribution creates a second trade-off. Static block distribution has low coordination overhead but may leave some workers with disproportionately expensive tasks. Round-robin distribution spreads task positions across workers. Dynamic distribution assigns small chunks from a shared queue, which can improve balance but adds queue coordination overhead. The experiment investigates the combined effect of placement and distribution on a repeatable CPU-bound workload.)
/Dest [11 0 R /Fit]
/Parent 966 0 R
/Prev 1037 0 R
>>
endobj
